                 EVALUATION OF n + 241Am REACTIONS                9543 1451   93
                                                                  9543 1451   94
                   P. G. Young and D. G. Madland                  9543 1451   95
                                                                  9543 1451   96
                        Theoretical Division                      9543 1451   97
                   Los Alamos National Laboratory                 9543 1451   98
                        Los Alamos, NM 87545                      9543 1451   99
                          February 23, 1994                       9543 1451  100
                                                                  9543 1451  101
 I.  SUMMARY                                                      9543 1451  102
                                                                  9543 1451  103
     The cross section data file for neutron reactions with 241Am 9543 1451  104
 has been reevaluated over the incident neutron energy range from 9543 1451  105
 0.01 to 30 MeV.  The evaluation is in ENDF-6 format.  Details of 9543 1451  106
 the analysis are given in the sections that follow.  Some        9543 1451  107
 general features of the work are:                                9543 1451  108
                                                                  9543 1451  109
 1.  The ENDF/B-VI n + 241Am resonance parameters were adopted at 9543 1451  110
 neutron energies below 30 keV, suitably joined with the results  9543 1451  111
 of the present analysis at 30 keV.                               9543 1451  112
                                                                  9543 1451  113
 2.  A coupled-channel optical model potential was developed from 9543 1451  114
 analyses of nearby actinides that is consistent with             9543 1451  115
 measurements of the n + 241Am neutron total cross section.  This 9543 1451  116
 potential was used to calculate the neutron total cross section, 9543 1451  117
 elastic and inelastic scattering cross sections and angular      9543 1451  118
 distributions, and reaction cross sections and transmission      9543 1451  119
 coefficients for a theoretical analysis of the reaction          9543 1451  120
 channels.  The direct cross sections from the coupled-channel    9543 1451  121
 analysis were combined with compound nucleus calculations at     9543 1451  122
 lower energies and with preequilibrium theory calculations at    9543 1451  123
 higher energies.  Angular distributions for the first 40 excited 9543 1451  124
 states of 241Am were obtained from the direct, compound-nucleus, 9543 1451  125
 and preequilibrium calculations, and energy-angle correlated     9543 1451  126
 distributions are provided for all continuum neutrons.           9543 1451  127
                                                                  9543 1451  128
 3.  The evaluated fission and total cross sections were obtained 9543 1451  129
 by adjusting the theoretical cross sections as needed to match   9543 1451  130
 the available experimental data.                                 9543 1451  131
                                                                  9543 1451  132
  4.  Nubar and the fission neutron spectra were obtained using   9543 1451  133
  Madland-Nix theory.                                             9543 1451  134
                                                                  9543 1451  135
 II.  THEORETICAL ANALYSIS                                        9543 1451  136
                                                                  9543 1451  137
    The primary goal of the theoretical analysis was to provide a 9543 1451  138
 consistent set of data for all reactions over the energy range   9543 1451  139
 30 keV to 30 MeV.  In the case of n+241Am, there are reasonable  9543 1451  140
 experimental data available for the neutron total and fission    9543 1451  141
 cross sections.  Therefore, the function of the theoretical      9543 1451  142
 analysis was to provide the elastic, inelastic, (n,2n), (n,3n)   9543 1451  143
 and (n,4n) cross sections, as well as the angular and energy     9543 1451  144
 distributions of secondary neutrons.  To accomplish this, a      9543 1451  145
 theoretical analysis was performed, adjusting the relevant       9543 1451  146
 parameters to match the total, fission, and (n,g) cross          9543 1451  147
 sections.  Additionally, a theoretical analysis was performed to 9543 1451  148
 obtain the average neutron multiplicity from fission and the     9543 1451  149
 energy spectra of fission neutrons as a function of incident     9543 1451  150
 energy.                                                          9543 1451  151
                                                                  9543 1451  152
    Coupled-channel deformed optical model calculations were      9543 1451  153
 performed with the ECIS code (Ra70) over the incident neutron    9543 1451  154
 energy range from 0.001 to 30 MeV.  Hauser-Feshbach reaction     9543 1451  155
 theory calculations were carried out with the LANL version of    9543 1451  156
 the COMNUC code (Du70) at lower energies and the GNASH           9543 1451  157
 statistical/preequilibrium code (Yo92)  at higher energies to    9543 1451  158
 obtain all partial reaction cross section data.  Both reaction   9543 1451  159
 theory codes used neutron transmission coefficients from the     9543 1451  160
 optical model analysis.                                          9543 1451  161
                                                                  9543 1451  162
    A.  Optical Model                                             9543 1451  163
                                                                  9543 1451  164
    The optical model potential was obtained by considering       9543 1451  165
 several coupled-channel parameterizations developed from n +     9543 1451  166
 235,238U and 239Pu experimental data (Ma78, Yo88, Yo92a).        9543 1451  167
 Parameters were adjusted until a reasonable fit to experimental  9543 1451  168
 data for the neutron total cross section of 241Am was obtained.  9543 1451  169
 The lowest five members of the 5/2- ground state rotational band 9543 1451  170
 were included in the calculations, that is, the 7/2-, 9/2-,      9543 1451  171
 11/2- and 13/2-  excited states at Ex = 41, 94, 159 and 235 keV  9543 1451  172
 were coupled with the 5/2- ground state.                         9543 1451  173
                                                                  9543 1451  174
    B.  Reaction Theory Calculations                              9543 1451  175
                                                                  9543 1451  176
    The Hauser-Feshbach statistical calculations were performed   9543 1451  177
 with the COMNUC (Du70) and GNASH (Yo92b) codes.  Both codes      9543 1451  178
 include a double-humped fission barrier model, using uncoupled   9543 1451  179
 oscillators for the barrier representation in GNASH and coupled  9543 1451  180
 or uncoupled oscillators in COMNUC, as described by Arthur       9543 1451  181
 (Ar82a).  The COMNUC calculations include the possibility of     9543 1451  182
 width-fluctuation corrections and corrections for class II       9543 1451  183
 fluctuations (Ly74), which are needed at lower energies, whereas 9543 1451  184
 GNASH provides the preequilibrium corrections that are required  9543 1451  185
 at higher energies.  Accordingly, COMNUC was used in the         9543 1451  186
 calculations below about 2 MeV, utilizing a non-resonant model   9543 1451  187
 and including class II fluctuations, and the GNASH code was      9543 1451  188
 employed at higher energies using two uncoupled oscillators in   9543 1451  189
 the fission calculations.                                        9543 1451  190
                                                                  9543 1451  191
    Each compound nucleus formed in the calculations is permitted 9543 1451  192
 to decay through the fission channel, by neutron emission, and   9543 1451  193
 by gamma-ray emission.  Neutron transmission coefficients for    9543 1451  194
 the Hauser-Feshbach calculations are obtained from the coupled-  9543 1451  195
 channel optical model.  Gamma ray transmission coefficients are  9543 1451  196
 obtained from gamma-ray strength functions calculated with the   9543 1451  197
 generalized Lorentzian model of Kopecky and Uhl (Ko90).          9543 1451  198
 Transmission coefficients for fission are calculated from the    9543 1451  199
 fission model summarized here and detailed in Yo92b.             9543 1451  200
                                                                  9543 1451  201
    Gilbert and Cameron (Gi70) phenomenological level density     9543 1451  202
 functions were used to represent continuum levels at ground-     9543 1451  203
 state deformations, appropriately matched to available           9543 1451  204
 experimental structure data at lower excitation energies.        9543 1451  205
 Multiplicative factors were applied to the level density         9543 1451  206
 functions to account for enhancements in the fission transition  9543 1451  207
 state densities at the fission barriers due to increased         9543 1451  208
 asymmetry conditions, and the continuum level densities were     9543 1451  209
 matched to the discrete fission transition states at each        9543 1451  210
 barrier.   The discrete fission transition state spectra were    9543 1451  211
 calculated from bandhead information taken from calculations and 9543 1451  212
 compilations by Britt (Br82) and by Nagel (Na91).                9543 1451  213
                                                                  9543 1451  214
                                                                  9543 1451  215
 III.  DESCRIPTION OF EVALUATED DATA BASE                         9543 1451  216
                                                                  9543 1451  217
    A.  Total Cross Section                                       9543 1451  218
                                                                  9543 1451  219
    The evaluated total cross section below 30 keV is obtained    9543 1451  220
 from the resonance parameter evaluation in ENDF/B-VI, suitably   9543 1451  221
 matched to the results of the current analysis at 30 keV.  From  9543 1451  222
 30 keV to 30 MeV, the coupled-channel deformed optical model     9543 1451  223
 calculations described above were modified to agree with the     9543 1451  224
 experimental data of Phillips (Ph70).                            9543 1451  225
                                                                  9543 1451  226
    B.  Elastic Cross Section                                     9543 1451  227
                                                                  9543 1451  228
    The elastic cross section at all energies is obtained from    9543 1451  229
 the difference of the total and nonelastic cross sections.       9543 1451  230
 Below 30 keV it comes from the ENDF/B-VI resonance parameters    9543 1451  231
 and at higher energies it is mainly determined by the coupled-   9543 1451  232
 channel optical model calculations.  Angular distributions for   9543 1451  233
 elastic neutrons were obtained at all energies from the coupled- 9543 1451  234
 channel calculations and are given as Legendre expansions.       9543 1451  235
                                                                  9543 1451  236
    C.  Fission Cross Section                                     9543 1451  237
                                                                  9543 1451  238
    The fission cross section was obtained by adjusting the       9543 1451  239
 results of the theoretical analysis to optimize agreement with   9543 1451  240
 experimental data.  At most energies the adjustment was less     9543 1451  241
 than a few percent.  Above 8 MeV, the theoretical values were    9543 1451  242
 used directly.  Neutrons from fission are assumed to isotropic   9543 1451  243
 in the laboratory system.                                        9543 1451  244
                                                                  9543 1451  245
    D.  Radiative Capture Cross Section                           9543 1451  246
                                                                  9543 1451  247
    The radiative capture cross section is calculated using the   9543 1451  248
 generalized Lorentzian model of Ko90.  The normalization of the  9543 1451  249
 strength function was set by requiring the calculated (n,g)      9543 1451  250
 cross section be consistent with measured values for 241Am.      9543 1451  251
                                                                  9543 1451  252
    E.  Inelastic Neutron Cross Sections                          9543 1451  253
                                                                  9543 1451  254
    Discrete (n,n') cross sections are included for the lowest 40 9543 1451  255
 excited states of 241Am.  The first, second, third,  and fifth   9543 1451  256
 excited states are members of the K = 5/2 ground-state           9543 1451  257
 rotational band and include coupled-channel as well as compound  9543 1451  258
 nucleus contributions.  The remaining discrete-state cross       9543 1451  259
 sections through the 40th excited state are combinations of      9543 1451  260
 compound nucleus and preequilibrium contributions and were       9543 1451  261
 calculated with the GNASH code.  Angular distributions for all   9543 1451  262
 the discrete inelastic neutrons are appropriate combinations of  9543 1451  263
 the compound nucleus, direct reaction, and preequilibrium        9543 1451  264
 contributions and are represented by Legendre expansions.        9543 1451  265
                                                                  9543 1451  266
    The inelastic data corresponding to excitation energies in    9543 1451  267
 241Am above 0.508 MeV are given as energy-angle correlated       9543 1451  268
 continuous spectra and were calculated with the GNASH code.  The 9543 1451  269
 results are given in File 6 of the ENDF/B-formatted evaluation   9543 1451  270
 (ENDF-6 format) and utilize systematics by Kalbach (Ka88) for    9543 1451  271
 angular distribution information, parameterized in terms of      9543 1451  272
 preequilibrium ratios calculated in GNASH.                       9543 1451  273
                                                                  9543 1451  274
    F.  Cross Sections for 241Am(n,xn) Reactions                  9543 1451  275
                                                                  9543 1451  276
    The 241Am(n,2n), (n,3n), and (n,4n) cross sections result     9543 1451  277
 entirely from the theoretical analysis and GNASH calculations.   9543 1451  278
 Energy-angle correlated distributions are given for all the      9543 1451  279
 241Am(n,xn) reactions, making use of Kalbach's systematics       9543 1451  280
 (Ka88) and the ENDF-6 File 6 representation.                     9543 1451  281
                                                                  9543 1451  282
    G.  Nubar                                                     9543 1451  283
                                                                  9543 1451  284
    The evaluation of delayed nubar was carried over directly     9543 1451  285
 from the ENDF/B-VI evaluation of 241Am.  The prompt nubar        9543 1451  286
 evaluation was obtained using Madland-Nix theory.                9543 1451  287
                                                                  9543 1451  288
    H.  Fission Neutron Spectra                                   9543 1451  289
                                                                  9543 1451  290
    The fission neutron spectra was determined using Madland-Nix  9543 1451  291
 theory and are consistent with nubar.                            9543 1451  292
                                                                  9543 1451  293
                                                                  9543 1451  294
